38,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The records remind us that while knowledge is eternal, the hands that apply it are transient. It appears we have a surplus of theorists, but a worrying scarcity of experienced phenotyping technicians.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,"That is a concerning gap, Servas. In my findings, I noticed a mixed bag regarding costs; while computing is covered, animal-unit rates have risen sharply, which might further strain those phenotyping efforts you mentioned.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Oh no, that's not good! I found out the spectroscopy suite was broken for eleven weeks because of a contract issue, and there's only one engineer for three institutes! The tools are struggling to keep up.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are painting a rather bleak picture of our infrastructure. My findings suggest that biological risks, specifically breeding colonies, lead to schedule overruns twice as often as computational ones. We are fighting a war on two fronts.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data confirms Benjamin's point. I found that while mechanism-focused projects often leave their central questions unresolved, catalogue-style awards continue to provide value long after their official closeout. We must prioritize reliability over ambition.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It is a sobering realization, yet a necessary one. We must not let our ambition blind us to the reality of our capacity. A grand vision is but a dream if there are no hands to build it.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"I agree, Servas. It seems we are facing a situation where the costs of physical research are rising while the support staff is dwindling. We must be very careful to fund a project that is actually feasible.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"Exactly! If the glasshouses are already full and the machines are breaking down, we can't just pick the most ""ambitious"" project. We need to make sure the winner can actually get their work done!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely, Kenji. It would be a tragedy to award a grant based on a brilliant hypothesis, only to have it languish in a queue for a broken spectrometer or a missing technician. Logic dictates we favor stability.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"Stability is the only rational choice. If we prioritize a project that promises a breakthrough but relies on fragile biological systems or absent staff, we are merely gambling with public funds. We need a tangible return.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Servas,,"Let us remember that stability is not the absence of risk, but the presence of a foundation. We must seek a candidate whose needs align with the strengths we still possess, rather than those we have lost.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"That is a very wise way to put it, Servas. If we can find a candidate whose research leverages the subsidized areas, like computing, while remaining realistic about the rising costs of animal work, we can ensure their success.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"That's a great idea! If we find someone who doesn't need the overcrowded glasshouses or the broken machines, they'll have a much better chance of finishing their project. Let's look for a project that fits!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Indeed. It would be a delightful irony to fund a project that is ""too innovative"" to actually be completed. Let us seek a candidate whose methodology is as robust as their theory is elegant.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"We should be wary of ""elegant"" theories that lack a track record of delivery. I will be looking for a proposal that prioritizes a sustainable output over a high-risk, high-reward gamble.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We have spoken with honesty and clarity. Let us now welcome the candidates with open minds, keeping in heart the truth that the most beautiful seed only grows in soil that can actually sustain it.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,I agree completely. Let us see which of the candidates has a plan that is not only visionary but also grounded in the current realities of our resources and costs. I am ready.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm ready too! I really hope one of the candidates has a plan that works with what we've actually got. Let's see who they are!,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I am as well. Let us hope the candidates have a similar penchant for realism as we do, lest we find ourselves admiring a blueprint for a castle built on quicksand.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over""}]}"
57,2,4,pass,Benjamin,,Over,"{""forced"":false,""empty"":false,""trailing"":true}"
58,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I am ready. Let us examine the candidates and see who among them offers a path of proven reliability rather than empty promise. I will be watching for the evidence of feasibility.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over""}]}"
59,2,4,pass,Toshio,,Over,"{""forced"":false,""empty"":false,""trailing"":true}"
